(6)  The University ensures that its academic staff are appropriately qualified for the level of their teaching responsibilities. This means that:
  1. staff who are responsible for the recruitment of subject teaching staff are appropriately experienced and qualified themselves, and familiar with the relevant requirements under the Standards;
  2. staff with academic coordination duties are qualified at an appropriately high level and their recruitment is overseen by a senior member of the School;
  3. a robust framework for the assessment of Professional Equivalence is applied for teaching staff who do not possess the requisite qualification for a given level of teaching according to [the Standards](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?standard=4);
  4. for staff who do not yet meet Professional Equivalence: 
    1. the range of teaching duties allocated is appropriately limited until equivalence is reached;
    2. professional development plans and appropriate supervision arrangements are established;
    3. such arrangements are regularly monitored and reviewed through the University’s performance management lifecycle;
  5. robust records of all staff qualifications, assessments of professional equivalence, and professional development plans as appropriate, are maintained in University record-keeping systems.


(7)  Academic staff are expected to engage in scholarly activities that enhance course and subject design, the quality of their teaching and the fulfilment of their professional goals. This means that:
  1. staff must be actively engaged in scholarship activities that: 
    1. maintain and advance disciplinary knowledge, including professional practice where relevant;
    2. inform and enhance pedagogical training and practice through continuous professional development in teaching and learning practice;
  2. staff are encouraged to pursue scholarship through their own channels as well as through professional development opportunities provided at school and University level;
  3. staff with academic leadership responsibilities must ensure that teaching staff are participating in scholarship activities that are at an appropriate level for their teaching duties and in a relevant disciplinary area.


(8)  The University supports the enhancement of the quality of teaching and learning at La Trobe through a range of initiatives and professional development programs. In addition to activities run by People & Culture and schools the [La Trobe Academy](https://www.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/careers/la-trobe-academy) fosters the scholarship of teaching and learning by:
  1. encouraging, supporting and contributing to a culture of scholarship and evidence-based practice within the organisation;
  2. fostering a culture of continuous improvement and academic leadership;
  3. monitoring the external higher education environment to inform the continued development and currency of programs, systems and processes;
  4. supporting professional development activities;
  5. providing mentorship and support for staff to secure research funding or external grants for teaching and learning focused projects;
  6. recognising and rewarding excellence;
  7. facilitating collaboration and the sharing of knowledge and practices;
  8. measuring the impact and effectiveness of initiatives on teaching and learning outcomes. 

[Top of Page](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/document/view.php?id=420#document-top)

(9)  Teaching staff should be actively engaged in academic scholarship that:
  1. enables them to keep abreast of and contribute to developments in the relevant disciplinary area;
  2. strengthens their teaching practice;
  3. aligns with the University’s strategic objectives and their individual career development goals.


(10)  The expectations for the level of engagement in academic scholarship, and the types of activities undertaken, will vary according to the level of appointment of individual academic staff, as outlined in the [Academic Performance Framework](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/307032/Academic-Performance-Framework.pdf) and associated guidelines.

(11)  Teaching staff may demonstrate the maintenance and advancement of disciplinary knowledge as follows:
  1. Teaching and Research staff are deemed as meeting the academic scholarship requirement in relation to disciplinary knowledge;
  2. Teaching-Focussed staff must demonstrate their continued currency with disciplinary knowledge through scholarly activity. 


(12)  Required scholarly activity can include, but is not limited to, the following:
  1. active participation (such as delivering a paper)in disciplinary conferences, workshops, and seminars;
  2. public engagement activities on relevant disciplinary topics, such as writing in The Conversation;
  3. contributions to non-peer reviewed publications;
  4. contributions to advances in professional practice, where relevant, such as active participation in professional organisations.


(13)  Graduate research students appointed to fixed term or ongoing teaching roles are deemed as meeting the requirement of maintaining contemporary disciplinary knowledge so long as they:
  1. are currently enrolled, or on Leave of Absence for less than one calendar year; and 
  2. are up-to-date with their progress milestones.

(14)  Professional development in learning and teaching practice is required of all teaching staff within the scope of this Procedure, and may be achieved through both formal and informal activities including, but not limited to engagement or enrolment in:
  1. professional development activities;
  2. a recognised qualification in learning and teaching, such as the [Graduate Certificate in Higher Education, Curriculum, Teaching and Learning](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/teaching-development-and-recognition/Graduate-Certificate-in-Higher-Education-Curriculum-Teaching-and-Learning) (staff link only);
  3. teaching-focussed Outside Studies Programs (OSP);
  4. Scholarship of Learning and Teaching practice;
  5. relevant [Communities of Practice](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/teaching-development-and-recognition/professional-learning-opportunities/communities-of-practice) (staff link only);
  6. peer review of teaching as a mentee.


(15)  In accordance with the [Higher Education Standards Guidance Note](https://www.teqsa.gov.au/guides-resources/resources/guidance-notes/guidance-note-scholarship), SoLT is not:
  1. personal or professional development activities that constitute lower-level training or that are unrelated to the teaching role (e.g., training in unrelated software or research techniques, or taking a course in an unrelated field);
  2. membership of academic governance committees;
  3. dated or irrelevant scholarly activity;
  4. research in disciplines or fields not strategically aligned with the School’s or University’s goals;
  5. basic or routine practice that does not engage with advances in practice, or that is unrelated to the content or teaching methods of the discipline/field being taught;
  6. involvement in professional/community groups or activities unrelated to the content of the teaching methods of the discipline/field being taught;
  7. similar activities at a lower AQF level;
  8. simple attendance at conferences or membership of a professional body.

(20)  In partnership with the Office of the Provost and schools, the [La Trobe Academy](https://www.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/careers/la-trobe-academy) (the Academy) systematically supports the scholarship of learning and teaching through undertaking or facilitating the following activities:
  1. the granting of awards and recognition including internal and external awards and Fellowships of the Higher Education Academy (HEA);
  2. qualifications such as the [Graduate Certificate in Higher Education, Curriculum, Teaching and Learning](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/teaching-development-and-recognition/Graduate-Certificate-in-Higher-Education-Curriculum-Teaching-and-Learning) (staff link only) and microcredentials;
  3. professional development activities including workshops, seminars, and [introduction to teaching modules ](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/teaching-development-and-recognition/professional-learning-opportunities/teaching-induction-modules)(staff link only);
  4. knowledge exchange events as such the Learning and Teaching Forum and other planned seminars;
  5. coordination of the Peer Review of Teaching (PRT) Program;
  6. coordination of university-level communications and processes to raise the awareness of programs offered through the Academy and increase academic staff participation;
  7. industry engagement to establish and reaffirm connected communities and ensure La Trobe is recognised as global thought leaders in education.

(33)  For the purposes of this Procedure:
  1. academic scholarship: scholarship that maintains and advances disciplinary knowledge, including professional practice where relevant; and scholarship that supports continuing professional development in teaching and learning practice;
  2. discipline: Field of Education in accordance with the [Australian Standard Classification of Education](https://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs@.nsf/0/E7779A9FD5C8D846CA256AAF001FCA5C);
  3. Scholarship of Learning and Teaching (SoLT): systematic, critically reflective and evidence-based learning and teaching practice and/or leadership and/or pedagogic research. It is grounded in the relevant disciplinary and pedagogical literature, is subject to peer review, and contributes positively to continuous improvement in the student experience and the quality of teaching and assessment.

