(4)  Each person covered by this Policy is expected to:
  1. always act in the best interests of the University;
  2. avoid Conflicts of Interest.


(5)  A Conflict of Interest occurs when an individual’s personal interests compete with, could potentially compete with, or be reasonably perceived to compete with, the interests of the University.
(6)  A personal interest is any interest an individual covered by this Policy may have, other than the professional duties and responsibilities they owe to the University. Personal interests therefore include any type of benefit (whether financial or not) to:
  1. the individual themselves;
  2. persons with who the individual has a familial relationship (e.g. immediate and extended family);
  3. persons with who the individual has a personal relationship (e.g. close friends or intimate partners, or the family members of such individuals), former colleagues or graduate research students that an individual may have supervised); 
  4. other organisations/third parties to which an individual may owe a competing duty or seek to inappropriately benefit.


(7)  Conflicts of Interest can be actual, potential or perceived:
  1. an actual conflict of interest is where there is a conflict between an individual’s personal interest(s) and the interests of, or duties/responsibilities owed to, the University;
  2. a potential conflict of interest is when an individual’s personal interest(s) has the capacity to conflict with the interests of, or duties/responsibilities owed to, the University; and
  3. a perceived conflict of interest is when it is reasonable for people to believe that an individual’s personal interest could compete with the interests of, or duties/responsibilities owed to, the University.


(8)  Conflicts of Interest can exist or arise in a range of circumstances, including:
  1. recruitment;
  2. procurement;
  3. research;
  4. when an individual has or develops: 
    1. a personal relationship or familial relationship (e.g. between staff, staff and students and staff and contractors etc); 
    2. a relationship with an organisation/third-party and the organisation/third party already has or develops a relationship with the University.
  5. the receipt of benefits, such as gifts and hospitality; 
  6. outside work (whether paid or unpaid);
  7. a conflict of duty, which can often arise when people hold local government, Council or Board positions, for example: 
    1. when a person has access to the University’s confidential information, and it may benefit a third-party to which the person also owes a professional/fiduciary obligation; or 
    2. when a person undertakes lobbying on behalf of the third-party or takes any other steps intended to benefit that third party, in a non-transparent manner.


(9)  This University expects the highest standards and conduct and integrity in relation to Conflicts of Interest. Each person covered by this Policy must:
  1. avoid Conflicts of Interest;
  2. declare in advance and actively manage Conflicts of Interest appropriately, and in accordance with this Policy;
  3. decline all offers of gifts, benefits and hospitality other than in accordance with Part C of this Policy;
  4. follow any direction(s) given by a University representative in line with this Policy to avoid or manage a Conflict of Interest to the University’s reasonable satisfaction (including providing information and documentation as requested).


(10)  In addition, some individuals are required to disclose certain personal interest(s), regardless of whether they constitute a Conflict of Interest, in accordance with various legislative obligations. For example:
  1. Council members - [La Trobe University Act 2009 (Vic)](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?legislation=8);
  2. all staff in respect to being transparent about foreign influence - [Foreign Influence Transparency Scheme Act 2018](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?legislation=143) (Cth).


(11)  A failure to avoid, declare and/or appropriately manage a Conflict of Interest, or otherwise comply with this Policy, may result in internal disciplinary action. In serious cases, referral to public agency bodies, such as the Independent Broad-based Anti-corruption Commission (IBAC) may also be made.
