  4. failures on the part of a third party to ensure that course delivery meets the University’s obligations under legal and regulatory frameworks such as the [TEQSA Act](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?legislation=79), the [Higher Education Standards Framework](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?standard=4), [ESOS legislation](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?legislation=57) and the [Competition and Consumer Act](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?legislation=27).

(15)  As part of the preliminary development process, and before engaging Legal Services, the relevant Contract Sponsor or Nominee must:
  1. determine with the partner the desired features of the arrangement;
  2. engage with relevant school leadership for academic arrangements and approvals;
  3. consult with Quality and Standards, or Office of the Provost for clinical education agreements, regarding the documentation required in addition to the agreement (see [Process Frameworks](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/educational-partnerships)); 
  4. follow the detailed steps outlined in the [Process Frameworks](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/learning-and-teaching/educational-partnerships) for each type of educational partnership using the relevant forms and templates.

(32)  Agreements may be terminated, in consultation with Quality and Standards, and following advice from Legal Services, under the following circumstances:
  1. by mutual agreement of the parties;
  2. under the termination provisions outlined in the agreement or applicable schedules;
  3. following a report of a substantive breach of the agreement;
  4. at the direction of the University Senior Executive Group or Academic Board.


(33)  Initial discussions in relation to terminating an agreement will occur between the Contract Sponsor or Nominee and the partner.

(42)  An Academic Program Director (APD) must be appointed for each course prior to delivery. The APD is responsible for oversight of the day-to-day delivery of a course, annual and interim course reporting, and escalating issues as necessary to the agreement Nominee, Contract Sponsor or Quality and Standards.
(43)  A Joint Management Committee (JMC) is convened a minimum of once per year for each third-party teaching partnership under standard Terms of Reference. The JMC is responsible for oversight of the quality and strategic development of courses under the partnership via course and site review reports. Specific quality improvement actions may be required of, or instigated by, the JMC as a result of quality monitoring activities.
