(4)  The Executive Director, Asset Transformation is responsible for ensuring: 
  1. a safe system for entry and the conduct of tasks within Confined Spaces is established; and
  2. the effective management of all activities associated with entry and exiting Confined Spaces.


(5)  The Infrastructure and Operations Senior Leadership Team are responsible for ensuring:
  1. all facilities under their control implement a safe system for entry, exit and the conduct of tasks within Confined Spaces; 
  2. delegation of accountability for entry and activities conducted within Confined Spaces; 
  3. assign appropriate permit authority for Confined Space works; and
  4. the implementation of process to monitor, audit and review on the compliance with the safe system of Confined Space works.


(6)  The Health and Safety (H&S) team is responsible for: 
  1. periodically monitoring, reviewing and updating this Procedure. 
  2. providing compliance advice and support on this Procedure.


(7)  Persons in control of Confined Space work are responsible for ensuring: 
  1. a risk assessment is completed and risks are adequately controlled prior to entering into Confined Spaces;
  2. all persons associated with the work are advised and understand the contents of the risk assessment;
  3. methods are implemented to prevent persons without Confined Space Permits from entering Confined Spaces;
  4. the planning and return of Confined Space Permits and other required permits are properly coordinated; 
  5. persons conducting confined space work and relevant works (e.g. supervisor, standby person, emergency response team) are formally trained and assessed as competent; 
  6. all records relating to entry, exit and activities conducted within Confined Spaces are accurately maintained and readily available; 
  7. processes are in place to monitor the compliance with and effectiveness of the Confined Space system; 
  8. atmospheric testing is carried out appropriately by a competent person prior to work commencing; 
  9. team members associated with the confined space work are briefed on the risk and control measures and emergency procedures for the job.
  10. emergency response procedures are established for Confined Space works;
  11. emergency response procedures are carried out immediately after an emergency arises in Confined Spaces;
  12. emergency response procedures are rehearsed by relevant persons having roles in Confined Space works.


(8)  The Permit Authority’s role and responsibilities include:
  1. Participate in the risk assessment process;
  2. Review and confirm the adequacy of the requestor’s permit request;
  3. Communicate with other work related areas for interface hazards;
  4. Review and verify with the Permit Holder, the implementation of agreed controls;
  5. Review and verify with the Permit Holder, the permit conditions and other associated controls;
  6. Manage other Permit To Work (PTW) essentials, such as, suspension, handover and emergencies;
  7. Verify that permitted work sites are safe at the time the permit is handed back at the end of the work and that the PTW is closed and cancelled as required.


(9)  In accordance with Part D, the La Trobe University Manager who engages the contractor will be responsible for the following tasks associated with Confined Space work undertaken by contractors:
  1. verify a risk assessment has been completed by the contractor with LTU consultation and the controls are implemented before confined space work begins.
  2. where contractor Health and Safety management systems are used to control work in confined spaces, verify that they meet or exceed the minimum standards set by this Procedure.
  3. ensure all potential energy sources associated with the confined space are isolated prior to entry.
  4. identifying any simultaneous operations that may affect the confined space work and de-conflicting any overlaps.
  5. consulting with contractors to establish emergency management planning.
  6. auditing and reviewing confined space work undertaken by contractors to ensure controls are implemented and in-line with the risk assessment.
  7. upon completion of the work, confirm all personnel have exited the confined space, confirm that the equipment is in a safe condition to be made available for service, and that all permits associated with the confined space work have been closed.

(20)  All persons with tasks associated with confined spaces will be trained and assessed as competent to conduct those tasks. Persons will be reassessed at appropriate intervals to maintain their competency. This training will be recorded in the appropriate system at LTU:
  1. Contractors – Training will be verified upon issue of a confined space permit by the permit issuer (either LTU or contractor permit issuer).
  2. LTU Employees – These must be recorded in the area database for staff competencies and verified upon issue of the permit.


(21)  All personnel associated with confined space work will be required to have successfully completed the relevant national unit of competency via statement of attainment for enter and work in Confined Spaces. 
(22)  In addition to the national units of competency, adequate instruction and training must be provided to persons in:
  1. hazards of the Confined Space;
  2. appropriate use of control measures;
  3. the selection, use, fit, testing and storage of any use of personal protective equipment;
  4. the content of confined space permit;
  5. emergency procedures.

(23)  Emergency response procedures must be developed, rehearsed and implemented for all confined space work. The emergency response procedures will include the procedures for:
  1. the rescue of any person from the Confined Space, and 
  2. first aid to be provided to any person in the Confined Space and after rescue from the Confined Space.


(24)  An emergency response team, including standby person, is to be in place and on hand for the duration of work while people are occupying a confined space. The emergency response team must be involved in the planning of confined space work. The emergency response team must be properly trained and assessed to be competent to conduct the emergency response activities in the Confined Space. 
(25)  The emergency response procedures will vary depending on the circumstances, however the procedures must consist of the following minimum elements:
  1. an effective method of communication to raise the alarm in the event of an emergency e.g. radio or telephone;
  2. identification of first aid personnel and provisions to be on hand for the work;
  3. how emergency vehicles and the emergency response team will access the Confined Space;
  4. how any casualties will be extracted from the confined space;
  5. a list of equipment necessary to perform the rescue;
  6. the proof that the relevant people (i.e. response team, people entering the Confined Space, standby people, supervisors) have rehearsed and understood the emergency plan.

(33)  Confined spaces will at all times be secured against unauthorised access. Sign posting should be permanently installed where a Confined Space is likely to be entered and where a risk assessment deems it necessary. 
(34)  Where entry and exit to a Confined Space is required, signs and protective barriers will be erected to prevent entry of persons not involved in the tasks prior to anyone entering. This must be planned in advance to ensure the correct equipment is available. Signage will comply with AS1319-1994. 
(35)  In addition to the abovementioned, the sign must:
  1. identify the Confined Space;
  2. notify that people must not enter the Confined Space without a Confined Space entry permit;
  3. be clear and prominently positioned for any period that there is work related to Confined Space.

(43)  For works in natural Confined Spaces (e.g. caves), additional requirements are to be in place.
(44)  Additional to the requirements in part C, the risk assessment for work in a natural Confined Space must consider additional hazards and risks arising from:
  1. Fauna and flora of that confined space area and surrounding areas;
  2. Distribution of other biological hazards (e.g. virus, bacteria, fungus);
  3. Recent and forecasted weather conditions (e.g. rain, flood);
  4. Nature of surrounding areas that can increase the risk on entry, exit and work in the natural Confined Space;
  5. Physical abilities, skills, fitness level of persons entering the natural Confined Space;
  6. Likelihood to get lost inside the space;
  7. Likelihood to be stuck in the space (e.g. inside hole, trench, at height);
  8. Factors that may hinder the emergency procedure.


(45)  Person(s) who prepare risk assessments must take reasonably practicable steps to gather information about factors that can introduce or impact hazards and risks in the natural Confined Space.
(46)  Additional to requirements in part G, initial atmospheric test should be done remotely from outside the natural confined space as far as is reasonably practicable.
(47)  If it is necessary to enter the space to conduct initial atmospheric test, air-supplies respiratory equipment should be used by person conducting the test. The entry must only be undertaken after ensuring appropriate methods in place to control identified risks. 
(48)  After entering the natural confined space, continuous atmospheric monitoring must be carried out by at least one competent person for each work group.
(49)  A reasonable number of people for each work group must be agreed on before work commences. A person must not enter the natural Confined Space alone.
(50)  Each work group must have at least one person with competency in providing first aid.
(51)  Standby person(s) to be outside of the natural confined space and maintain continuous communication with people inside the space until work is completed and all people have exited the space.
(52)  Throughout the work, all people are responsible to make continuous assessment on whether they are able to continue or need to exit the natural confined space. 
(53)  Before commencing work in natural confined spaces, person managing the work must notify their responsible manager about the plan of the work (e.g. time, location, expected duration).

