(27)  Whilst driving a vehicle for university-related business, the driver will ensure the following:
  1. comply with all road laws and regulations;
  2. minimise driver distractions such as loud music, distracting conversation;
  3. secure goods to minimise the risk of objects striking occupants in the event of a collision;
  4. take regular stretch breaks and where possible, rotate drivers;
  5. notify on safe arrival for remote locations or where appropriate, with supervisors.


(28)  Driving whilst unfit is a health and safety risk. Each driver is responsible for assessing their own fitness to drive whilst on university-related business and in alignment with the [Workplace Behaviours Policy](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/document/view.php?id=87). Factors to consider include:
  1. not driving when feeling fatigued;
  2. blood alcohol concentration (BAC)0.00% whilst driving a University Fleet vehicle;
  3. not being under the influence of illicit drugs;
  4. being aware of the effects of prescribed medication and not driving if affected;
  5. being aware of any restrictions due to injury or illness and not driving if affected.


(29)  In situations when it is unsafe to continue driving, the driver will liaise with their supervisor and determine alternative arrangements. This may involve short-term accommodation for rest and recovery.

(30)  A submission for a higher doctorate will normally consist of:
