(6)  There are a number of areas of perceived, actual or potential conflicts of interest which may arise in the context of Outside Work for Professional Staff which are listed below. The list is not exhaustive and other situations of Conflict of Interest may arise and should be assessed on an individual basis. Conflict of Interest concerns may arise in the following situations:
  1. Outside Work undertaken during the same hours that a staff member is being paid to work for the University. The conflict of interest may still arise whether the work is undertaken at a University location or another location and where the Professional Staff Member is not engaged to perform work for the University or is on paid leave;
  2. the demands of the Outside Work compromises the staff member’s capacity or ability to perform their duties or meet their Workplace Health and Safety obligations within the University;
  3. Outside Work makes use of any University facility, equipment or resources including computer software and information technology resources;
  4. Outside Work makes use of, or may allow the University staff member to benefit from, commercial or other information that the University staff member possesses by virtue of their employment with the University;
  5. Outside Work is with an organisation that may compete with the University;
  6. Outside Work is with an organisation that supplies the University with equipment, services or staff; and
  7. Outside Work is with an organisation that is a customer of the University and may acquire equipment, services or staff from the University.

(7)  Undertaking Outside Work may have benefits for a Professional Staff Member and the University. The University does not preclude such arrangements from occurring, provided:
  1. the staff member discloses the arrangements and seeks approval from the University prior to the commencement of a period of Outside Work;
  2. the arrangements do not interfere with the staff member’s discharge of their professional duties or encroach unduly on work time;
  3. the arrangements do not create a conflict of interest with other obligations that cannot be actively and appropriately managed;
  4. the proposed activity is not contrary to the University’s interests, values or reputation; and
  5. Outside Work does not create a risk of foreign interference which cannot be appropriately managed. Areas which may be taken into account include: 
    1. whether the Outside Work is with a foreign government, political party, military, policing or security organisation;
    2. what the obligations are owed to foreign institutions;
    3. the nature of the role; and
    4. the payment (in any form) received.

