(4)  The University engages in the development and delivery of short courses to enhance its industry engagement, to develop the skills of individuals, and to contribute to the University’s revenue. This means that short courses are developed that:
  1. align with the strategic direction and strengths of the University and its individual schools and research centres;
  2. build critical skills for adult learners for professional, community and individual benefit;
  3. are assessed as being financially viable for the duration of their offer.


(5)  All short courses are designed in accordance with the [Short Course Design Framework](https://intranet.latrobe.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0019/323245/LTU-Short-Course-Design-Framework-August-v1.1.pdf) and must demonstrate contemporary academic and industry relevance. This means that:
  1. the design of short courses is informed by industry engagement and emerging scholarship;
  2. learning materials are designed and selected to support the application of the skill or knowledge by the participant, where appropriate;
  3. where aligned with an industry framework short courses meet registration and recognition requirements of those frameworks or accrediting bodies;
  4. short courses are supported by the availability of qualified instructors and appropriate learning environments.


(6)  Short course design takes account of the specific needs of target cohorts. This means that:
  1. the structure, delivery mode, timing and duration of short courses is tailored for target cohorts;
  2. short courses are designed as self-contained units of study;
  3. the prior knowledge or experience required by participants is clear;
  4. short course learning objectives are explicit, designed for the designated level of the course, and describe the proficiency to be gained by participants on completion;
  5. where short courses are assessed, the assessment is designed and conducted in accordance with the [Higher Education Standards Framework 2021](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?standard=4). Specifically assessment tasks must: 
    1. be linked with the learning objective(s);
    2. be commensurate with the volume of learning;
    3. support the application of the skills or knowledge;
  6. where a short course is also a microcredential the following information must be explicit: 
    1. the number of credit points allocated;
    2. any possibility for stacking and the amount of credit available towards relevant award courses.


(7)  The University assures the quality of its short courses through appropriate mechanisms for the type of course. This means that:
  1. the design and delivery of short courses is consistent with the [Higher Education Standards Framework 2021](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?standard=4);
  2. the initial proposal, business case and curriculum content for all short courses must be reviewed and approved by the designated authorities (see Part B of the Procedure);
  3. where a short course is a microcredential it aligns with the applicable published Australian frameworks or standards (currently the NMF);
  4. all short courses are subject to regular review through participant surveys and peer review and outcomes are used to inform the ongoing development of the course;
  5. short course participants are made aware of the requirement for academic honesty in any assessed work and the requirement for them to abide by the provisions in the [Student Academic Misconduct Policy](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/document/view.php?id=221).


(8)  The University seeks to maximise the experience of short course participants by:
  1. ensuring participants have timely access to quality learning resources and appropriate supports;
  2. providing clear information in advance to participants of their entitlements and obligations.


(9)  Contractual arrangements for the delivery of all short courses are conducted according to the University [Contracts Policy](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/document/view.php?id=30).
