  2. receive a course credit, or a full, partial or no refund contingent on meeting such terms and conditions.

(33)  Short course participants are advised of, and agree to, their specific entitlements and obligations through the registration in their short course. Participants are required to abide by all key University policies governing student and participant behaviours, and are entitled to the following:
  1. access to the La Trobe Learning Management System (LMS) and all learning materials required for their short course (individuals participating in short courses taught by La Trobe University are regarded as students for the purposes of access to learning materials under the University’s Statutory Licence (refer Part IVA Division 4 of the [Copyright Act 1968](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/directory/summary.php?legislation=1)) or otherwise access materials as ‘authorised users’of commercially licensed material;
  2. attendance or participation in teaching sessions and other scheduled activities for the duration of their short course or for a limited period by negotiation where required;
  3. academic guidance through drop-in sessions or other fora, and where the short course includes assessment, an assessment of their learning and performance;
  4. certification of their attendance or achievement according to the nature of their course;
  5. access to the Safer Community program for support should they encounter inappropriate, concerning, or unacceptable behaviour (bullying, harassment, discrimination, sexual harm, stalking, threats, or violence) from students or staff of the University while engaged in their short course. 


(34)  Participants who successfully complete short courses are considered short course alumni of the University.

(35)  Any short course information or promotional materials must not imply that short courses are awards of the University or recognised within the AQF. 
(36)  Prior to the commencement of a course, participants are provided with published information including:
  1. any applicable entry requirements (see Part D of this Procedure);
  2. information about course content;
  3. details of any assessment, timelines and intended learning outcomes;
  4. the nature of the certification to be provided on completion of the course;
  5. whether completion of the course may be considered for credit upon successful admission to an award course at La Trobe;
  6. relevant fees, any associated costs, and cancellation and refund policies;
  7. the rights and responsibilities of short course participants.


(37)  Where a short course is a microcredential, course information clearly identifies:
  1. the volume of credit available where a participant has successfully completed the assessment;
  2. the AQF level of the course;
  3. the potential award pathways where the microcredential can be used for credit, including where a microcredential must be stacked with another microcredential in order to be recognised for credit;
  4. where the microcredential is recognised by a professional body, satisfies, or aligns to an industry standard or professional development requirement, or constitutes recognition towards an industry or vendor certification.


(38)  Participants are provided with course information, content and learning materials two weeks prior to the commencement of a short course.
(39)  All communications with short course participants are sent through the email address nominated at the point of registration.

(58)  For the purposes of this Policy and Procedure:
  1. assessed short course: a course that is not an award of the University, but in which assessment attainment is recorded and certification of achievement is provided;
  2. bridging program: a short course which is designed to address knowledge gaps for adult learners seeking to enter a specific course. Bridging programs require assessment but are not credit bearing.
  3. Course Owner: the role or individual ultimately responsible for the content of a course. Where a course originates in a school this is the Dean (or nominee). In other instances this is the Provost, Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Academic) or nominee.
  4. digital badge: a digital representation that an individual has successfully completed a short course;
  5. digital credential: a form of certification that is awarded to an individual that has successfully completed a microcredential;
  6. domestic applicant: a student who is an Australian citizen, New Zealand citizen, the holder of a permanent visa for Australia or holder of an Australian humanitarian visa;
  7. international student: a student who is not a domestic student at the time of the relevant study, who will be studying in Australia on a temporary student visa;
  8. microcredentials: short courses consisting of multiples of five credit points that are assessed and which, in approved sequences, may be used to apply for credit towards an award of the University. In this Policy the term is also used to denote the qualification that is awarded on successful completion of the course.
  9. non-assessed course: a short course where there is no formal assessment, but for which a certificate of attendance may be issued;
  10. non-resident applicant: all applicants/students who are not classified as domestic or international as defined in this Policy in relation to their visa/citizenship and location status. Non-Residents include: 
    1. non-domestic students who are studying in a domestic course that is delivered entirely online, where they do not need a student visa for their enrolment;
    2. non-domestic students studying on campus within Australia while on any temporary visa other than the student visa;
    3. any student enrolled in a non-domestic course (including Australian domestic students studying overseas in a course delivered by an offshore partner institution);
  11. participant: an individual registered in a short course of the University;
  12. Professional Certificate: a non-AQF award of the University consisting of 30 credit points that may consist of subjects from an AQF award of the University and/or microcredentials;
  13. registration: the process by which an individual’s participation in a short course is confirmed;
  14. short course: non-award courses of short duration run by the University or with a third party that are designed for experienced learners for professional, individual or community benefit;
  15. Single Subject: a subject of an award course of the University in which a student enrols independently of a direct enrolment in the course and which may be used to apply for credit towards an award;
  16. stacking: the process of bundling microcredentials together, either vertically or horizontally to provide a pathway to an award course.

