(83)  The University encourages reporting of unacceptable behaviour and therefore provides a complaint procedure for dealing with complaints relating to workplace behaviours.
(84)  The person raising a complaint has the right to:
  1. have their concern/complaint acknowledged;
  2. seek support and assistance at any time during the resolution process;
  3. receive sensitive and timely responses from the person approached for assistance;
  4. have the matter dealt with discreetly and sensitively, using an impartial process;
  5. be advised about the resolution process;
  6. express concerns about unacceptable behaviour without fearing or experiencing negative consequences;
  7. discontinue the complaint with the knowledge that the University may continue to manage the complaint;
  8. know the findings in relation to whether the complaint has been substantiated or not upon completion of the University’s investigation; and
  9. lodge a complaint externally with a relevant authority.

(85)  The principles of the complaint resolution process are:
  1. providing timely responses;
  2. staff being dealt with sensitively;
  3. all parties are afforded fairness and impartiality;
  4. support provided for all parties;
  5. the resolution process is victimisation free; and
  6. the principles of natural justice and procedural fairness apply.

(89)  It is recommended where appropriate that staff initially try to resolve a complaint personally with the other party/parties. If the staff member is comfortable to try this approach, it is generally the most effective way to ensure the continuation of a harmonious working relationship.
(90)  Staff should consider lodging a formal complaint where:
  1. the staff member has explored and tried different ways of raising or confronting the issue without success;
  2. the issue has become untenable; for example, it involves others, divides people, and/or continues to grow unabated;
  3. the behaviour of another is having a negative impact on the staff member and continues without regard to the staff member’s expressed wishes;
  4. raising the issue with the supervisor informally would expose the staff member to further inappropriate behaviours;
  5. in the staff member’s judgment, this issue requires intervention at a more formal level; and
  6. the serious nature of the issue justifies or requires making a formal complaint.


(91)  The below process provides an opportunity to resolve the complaint in a timely and fair manner.

(99)  To ensure consistency and fairness, the below process is usually followed where a decision to investigate a complaint has been made by Human Resources (the order of events may change and an independent person who is external to the University may be used to conduct the investigation):
  1. the complainant (staff member lodging the complaint) is interviewed and the allegations are particularised in writing;
  2. the allegations are conveyed to the respondent (the person the complaint is about);
  3. the respondent is given the opportunity to respond to the allegations;
  4. if there is a dispute over facts, statements from any witnesses and other relevant evidence is gathered;
  5. a finding is made as to whether the complaint has substance; and
  6. the decision-maker reviews the finding and determines outcome/s.


(100)  Notwithstanding the above, if at any stage during the investigation process it is identified that continuing or fixed-term staff member has allegedly engaged in misconduct or serious misconduct, then the matter must proceed in accordance with misconduct and serious misconduct procedures outlined in clause 67 of the [Enterprise Agreement](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/download.php?id=422&version=2&associated), or the staff member’s contract of employment.

(104)  There are a number of potential outcomes that may result from resolving complaints. These will depend on factors such as:
  1. the severity and frequency of the behaviour;
  2. the weight of the evidence;
  3. the wishes of the staff member lodging the complaint;
  4. whether the staff member who is the subject of the complaint could have been expected to know that such behaviour was a breach of policy;
  5. whether there have been any prior incidents or warnings;
  6. whether misconduct or serious misconduct has been substantiated.


(105)  The outcomes are not limited to, but may include:  
| No Action  | If the initial assessment and/or investigation identifies that the complaint is not substantiated.  |  
| --- | --- |  
| Coaching  | Informal action through coaching. If the initial assessment and/or investigation identifies that the behaviour is not serious enough to warrant disciplinary action but may find that a restatement of policy and standards expected, further instruction, re-education or training and coaching the staff member is appropriate.  |  
| Restatement of Expected Behaviours  | If the staff member is aware of behaviour expectations but has not followed correct procedures and/or behaviour standards, a restatement of expected behaviours will be issued and consequences of repeated instances of such behaviour will be advised.  |  
| Disciplinary Action  | If the staff member commits a serious breach of policy/procedure and/or misconduct or serious misconduct is established, the University can issue a formal censure or counselling in conjunction with a written warning; withhold an increment, demote by one or more classifications or increments, transfer to another position with pay appropriate to the position; suspend with or without pay, physical relocation at the staff member’s campus location having regard to the nature of the conduct, suspension of access to certain University locations or equipment, and termination of employment.  |  
| Termination of Employment  | Means termination of employment at the initiative of the University in accordance with relevant clauses of the [Enterprise Agreement](https://policies.latrobe.edu.au/download.php?id=422&version=2&associated) and/or the staff member’s contractual terms.  |  
